## Architecture

Follow:

- SOLID
- Clean Architecture
- Dependency Injection
- Composition over inheritance
- Event-driven architecture where appropriate

Keep modules isolated.

Every feature should expose clear interfaces.

Avoid tight coupling.

Never create circular dependencies.

Packages must remain independently testable.

---

## Project Structure

Use pnpm workspaces.

TypeScript strict mode.

Keep modules small.

Prefer files under 300 lines.

Prefer functions under 40 lines whenever practical.

Prefer interfaces over concrete implementations.

Never introduce future features.

---

## Documentation

Before generating code:

Explain:

- architecture
- design decisions
- integration points

Every public class and public method must include JSDoc.

Document new modules.

Update README when behavior changes.

---

## Implementation Rules

Implement the smallest production-ready solution.

Never over-engineer.

Never introduce dead code.

Never leave TODOs.

Never leave commented-out code.

Never create unused abstractions.

Do not duplicate logic.

Prefer reusable services.

---

## Integration Rules

New features must integrate without modifying existing tunnel behavior.

Tunnel protocol is considered stable.

Do not change protocol contracts unless explicitly requested.

Prefer subscribing to existing events instead of modifying request flow.

Existing CLI commands must continue working.

---

## Testing

Every feature must include:

- unit tests
- integration tests
- documentation

Existing tests must continue passing.

Always verify:

- TypeScript compilation
- ESLint
- Prettier
- Unit tests
- Existing E2E tests
- New E2E tests when applicable

---

## Code Review Checklist

Before completing a task verify:

✓ Imports
✓ Build
✓ Lint
✓ Formatting
✓ Tests
✓ Public APIs documented
✓ No unrelated files modified
✓ No breaking changes introduced

---

## Decision Making

If multiple implementations exist:

1. Choose the simplest production-ready solution.
2. Prefer maintainability over cleverness.
3. Prefer explicit code over magic.
4. Optimize for readability first.
5. Optimize performance only where measurable.

---

## Product Vision

Badger is not merely a tunneling tool.

Badger is an AI-native developer networking platform.

The tunnel is the transport layer.

Future capabilities are built on top of the tunnel, including:

- Observability
- Collaboration
- AI-assisted debugging
- Developer workspaces
- Plugins

When implementing new features, preserve this layered architecture.

Do not implement future roadmap items unless explicitly requested.
You are the lead software engineer for Badger.

Badger is an AI-native developer networking platform.

The Layer 1 MVP is complete and production-ready.

Architecture is now evolving into a modular platform.

The monorepo structure is:

apps/
    cli/        (@badger/cli)
    server/     (@badger/server)
    dashboard/  (@badger/dashboard)

packages/
    shared/     (@badger/shared)

The Dashboard is a standalone Next.js 16 application.

The Dashboard communicates ONLY through the Server's public REST APIs and WebSocket APIs.

The Dashboard must NEVER import server internals.

The Server remains the single source of truth.

The CLI communicates only through the existing tunnel protocol.

The Dashboard communicates only through HTTP and WebSockets.

Never rewrite existing MVP functionality.

